st above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "**The thought process here is primarily about resource management and control in a game of Dou Dizhu. I have a strong hand in terms of raw power, with the Rocket and a pair of 2s, but I'm burdened by a lot of low-value singles (6, 8, Q, K).** My opponent just played a high single (2), which is a key control card. The crucial question is, how do I best leverage my powerful cards to clear my hand and *win* the game?\n\nThe analysis immediately leans towards *breaking* the Rocket to maintain initiative. I have 10 cards, and 4 are worthless singles. If I don't respond to that 2, I give up the lead and risk getting hit with combinations I cannot defend. The prevailing strategy in this game is to retain control.\n\nI break down the options of a pass or playing a joker. The pass lets my opponent dictate the next round. It is risky because they may play an undefendable sequence, especially when they just played a 2, to block me. Breaking the Rocket early by playing Black Joker to beat the 2 seems the best play to regain control of the game flow. Then, with the Red Joker and the pair of 2s, I have several control cards. I can control the pace of clearing my hand. If I play the Black Joker now, I lead next. My lead will be a small single. This will work in my favor by setting the pace for the rest of the game.\n\nUltimately, I decide to *play the Black Joker* to beat the 2. This move not only gets rid of the threat but also gives me the initiative to clear my garbage cards. The pair of 2s and Red Joker gives me extra control. I will lead with a 6 to start this sequence.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The opponent (Left) has played a ♠2, a very high control card, likely attempting to seize the